% ============================================================================
% sedimentary-rock-formation-step — the THREE-stage process by which
% sedimentary rock forms, each stage's step number (adj-facts-stdlib,
% EARTH-SCIENCE).
% ============================================================================
% The THIRD instance of the "Earth-processes" Major Gap
% (ADJ-STDLIB-COVERAGE.md), after `geology/earth-layer-matter-behavior.adj`
% and `weathering-cause-type.adj`, and a genuinely NEW axis from every rock
% table already shipped in this stdlib: `geology/rock-type.adj` and
% `rock-types.adj` each name WHAT a rock type forms FROM in one summary
% phrase (sedimentary → "deposited_weathered_material" /
% "compacted_sediment"); `weathering-cause-type.adj` sorts individual
% weathering CAUSES into physical/chemical; `metamorphism-cause.adj` sorts
% metamorphism CAUSES into a shared effect. None of them states the ORDER
% the process actually runs in — this table does, the same "named stage → a
% NUMBER marking its position" shape `water-cycle.adj` already established
% for a different Earth cycle. Recall is a binding query:
%
%     ? sedimentary_rock_formation_step(erosion, $N)   % 2
%
% A native `table` (ADJ-TABLES RS-5): each `row` lowers to a ground relation
% `sedimentary_rock_formation_step(stage, step_number)` carrying the
% citation, so the lookup above is the ordinary SLD binding query — the
% engine returns the step WITH its source, on the CPU, with zero model
% calls, and abstains on anything the source does not name as one of these
% three stages. The `step_number` value is a NUMBER, so a recalled step
% composes into arithmetic, exactly like `water-cycle.adj`'s own
% `step_number` column. The query also runs BACKWARD — bind the number and
% recall which stage holds that position:
%
%     ? sedimentary_rock_formation_step($S, 3)   % compaction
%
% The three stages, as a little truth table (the source's own three
% back-to-back sentences, one continuous paragraph, each handing off to the
% next):
%
%     stage        step_number   the source's own words
%     -----------  ------------  ------------------------------------------
%     weathering   1             "begins with the weathering, or breaking
%                                 down, of the exposed rock into small
%                                 fragments"
%     erosion      2             "these fragments are removed from their
%                                 source and transported ... to a new
%                                 location"
%     compaction   3             "the lowest layers become compacted so
%                                 tightly that they form solid rock"
%
% ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
% Provenance (byte-quotable, per the ADJ-facts standard).
% ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
% curl-fetched the raw National Geographic Education "The Rock Cycle" page
% directly (not just an AI-summarized WebFetch) and confirmed byte-for-byte,
% inside the page's own embedded article JSON, the one continuous paragraph
% under its "Clastic and Organic Sedimentary Rocks" heading:
%
%   "The formation of clastic and organic rocks begins with the weathering,
%    or breaking down, of the exposed rock into small fragments. Through the
%    process of erosion, these fragments are removed from their source and
%    transported by wind, water, ice, or biological activity to a new
%    location. Once the sediment settles somewhere, and enough of it
%    collects, the lowest layers become compacted so tightly that they form
%    solid rock."
%
% `trust consensus` — National Geographic Education, the SAME source family
% already cited at this tier by `biology/consumer-trophic-level.adj`
% (Thread 6 of this loop's session log), a reputable, widely-used K-8
% science-education publisher, not a primary government research source.
%
% WHY this table is honestly SCOPED to the sedimentary path, not named
% "rock-cycle" generally: the SAME cited page also states how igneous rock
% forms ("formed when molten hot material cools and solidifies") and how
% metamorphic rock forms ("changed from their original form by immense heat
% or pressure") — but neither of those is a multi-stage, orderable SEQUENCE
% the way the sedimentary path is; each is a single combined transformation
% with no second or third numbered stage the source names. Only the
% sedimentary path gets a three-sentence, hand-off-style paragraph a step
% count can honestly be built from — so this table's own predicate name
% says exactly what it grounds, no more.
%
% HONEST ABSTENTION:
%
% 1. `deposition` is deliberately NOT a row, even though many other K-8
%    science sources use it as a named fourth stage between erosion and
%    compaction. This page's own paragraph never uses the word — it says
%    fragments are "transported ... to a new location" and the "sediment
%    settles somewhere", folding what other sources call "deposition" into
%    the erosion/compaction stages' own prose rather than naming it as its
%    own numbered step. A query for `deposition`'s step honestly abstains
%    rather than inventing a fourth stage this source never states.
% 2. `melting` is deliberately NOT a row here either, despite being a real
%    term the SAME page uses elsewhere (for how igneous rock forms). It is
%    not part of the sedimentary path's own three-sentence paragraph, so a
%    query for it against THIS predicate abstains — the honest boundary
%    between what this table grounds (the sedimentary sequence) and what a
%    sibling table would need to ground separately (the igneous path).
%
% (See ../README.md; feedback_nothing_human_authored,
% feedback_adj_only_shipped_artifacts, feedback_no_byte_arithmetic_for_llm.)
% ============================================================================

table sedimentary_rock_formation_step {
    columns stage, step_number

    row (weathering, 1)
    row (erosion, 2)
    row (compaction, 3)

    source "The formation of clastic and organic rocks begins with the weathering, or breaking down, of the exposed rock into small fragments."
    locator "https://education.nationalgeographic.org/resource/rock-cycle/"
    trust consensus

    cites "Through the process of erosion, these fragments are removed from their source and transported by wind, water, ice, or biological activity to a new location." locator "https://education.nationalgeographic.org/resource/rock-cycle/"
    cites "Once the sediment settles somewhere, and enough of it collects, the lowest layers become compacted so tightly that they form solid rock." locator "https://education.nationalgeographic.org/resource/rock-cycle/"
}
